(project pricklypear-image
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: image")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section image
  (doc "The per-user unikernel: Lisp interpreter, REPL endpoint, htmx interface")
  (status partial)
  
  (subsection boot
   (doc "Reading the manifest, loading initial env")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_img_it7.py —   image boots + serves / and /health + REPL accepts connections"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         On boot, the image:
         1. Reads its boot manifest (from common/lib/manifest.ml
            type, fetched from Postgres by the supervisor
            before booting).
         2. Manifest specifies:
            - user_id (who this image is; asserted by
              supervisor after auth verified the
              credential — see section auth. The image
              trusts this because supervisor+auth did the
              verification; the image does no auth itself.)
            - username (for the REPL prompt and author
              tracking)
            - branch_id (default branch, usually user/main)
            - boot_mode ('on-connect' | 'always-on')
            - image_version (for A/B tracking)
            - config (budgets, feature flags)
         3. Loads all function_defs and type_defs for
            branch_id from Postgres into the in-memory
            env (or, with no-caching policy, sets up the
            branch-aware lookup that reads on each call).
         4. Starts the REPL endpoint (internal line
            protocol; the supervisor forwards SSH channels
            here — see ssh-interface). NOT a full SSH server.
         5. Starts the htmx server (httpaf-lwt-unix in v1)
            on configured port.
         6. Registers health endpoint.
         7. Marks self healthy, signals supervisor.

         The image is STATELESS across reboots w.r.t. user
         data: anything that must survive is in Postgres.
         In-memory REPL state (define'd locals in a
         session) does NOT survive reboot unless the user
         persisted via (store) or similar.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection ssh-interface
   (doc "The SSH front door: supervisor authenticates, forwards to image REPL")
   (implements supervisor/lib/ssh.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_awa.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_awa_shims.ml supervisor/lib/ssh_keys.ml supervisor/lib/boot.ml)
   (status deprecated)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — line-protocol SSH→auth→image→REPL→define→call")
    (smoke "PP_AWA_SSH_PORT=12223 + real ed25519 key in DB → awa-ssh handshake + pubkey auth"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE SSH PATH (end to end):

         1. User runs: ssh alice@pricklypear.host
         2. SUPERVISOR listens on port 22 (awa-ssh,
            Mirage-compatible) and accepts the connection.
         3. SSH handshake + key exchange (awa-ssh, pure
            OCaml, no libssh).
         4. Authentication: supervisor calls pricklypear
            authd (NOT wyo.auth — deliberate v1 choice;
            see workflow.conventions) via verify_ssh_key
            RPC to resolve the presented public key to a
            user_id. Key -> user mapping is authd's job.
         5. Supervisor boots (boot-on-connect) or attaches
            (always-on) the user's image unikernel.
         6. Supervisor opens a channel to the image's
            internal REPL endpoint (see repl_endpoint
            below) and FORWARDS the SSH channel's PTY
            stream to it. The user's terminal is now
            talking to the image's REPL.
         7. Image presents the REPL prompt (see
            repl-experience).
         8. On disconnect, supervisor notes session end;
            boot-on-connect images die, always-on images
            keep running.

         WHY SUPERVISOR-FRONTS-SSH (not each image on its
         own port):
         - One door, one auth, one image per user. Matches
           'SSH-first OS' — it's THE login, not N logins.
         - Users don't need to know their image's port.
         - authd integration happens in one place
           (supervisor), not N places (each image).
         - The image doesn't need full SSH — just a REPL
           endpoint. Simpler image.

         TRADEOFF: supervisor-to-image hop is internal
         (trusted hypervisor network), so no end-to-end
         SSH crypto between user terminal and image. For
         a personal OS on a trusted hypervisor, this is
         acceptable. If it becomes a concern, the image
         can run its own awa-ssh on an internal port and
         the supervisor does TCP forwarding of the
         encrypted SSH stream (then the image decrypts).
         Defer this until it's a felt concern.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection repl-endpoint
   (doc "Image's internal REPL endpoint, not full SSH")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_persist_it8.py — define persists + /tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — REPL proxy works"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The image does NOT run a full SSH server. Instead
         it exposes an internal REPL endpoint — a simple
         line protocol over a TCP socket (or Mirage
         channel) on an internal port. The supervisor
         connects to this and forwards the user's PTY
         stream.

         Protocol (minimal, framed):
           - Connect: supervisor opens TCP to image:REPL_PORT
           - Auth: none needed (internal network; the
             supervisor already authenticated the user)
           - Exchange: lines of UTF-8. User input flows
             in; REPL output flows out.
           - The image's session.ml tracks this connection
             as a session; on disconnect, session ends.

         WHY NOT FULL SSH IN THE IMAGE:
         - Removes awa-ssh dependency from the image
           (keeps image smaller; awa crypto is non-trivial).
         - One SSH implementation (in supervisor) to
           maintain, not N.
         - authd resolution stays in supervisor.

         The endpoint is in image/lib/repl_endpoint.ml.
         It owns: accepting the supervisor's connection,
         wiring PTY stream to the line editor (lineedit.ml),
         and the REPL read-eval-print loop itself.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection repl-experience
   (doc "What the user sees: prompt, multi-line, line editing, meta-commands")
   (implements image/lib/lineedit.ml)
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_e2e_it6.py — define + apply works through the REPL"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE PROMPT:
           alice/main>           -- showing user + current branch
           alice/main (def) |     -- mid-multiline-define

         READING INPUT:
         - The REPL reads s-expressions. It keeps reading
           lines until parens are balanced and strings
           closed (standard Lisp REPL behavior). The
           reader tells us 'incomplete' so we know to
           prompt for more, not to evaluate yet.
         - One REPL turn = one s-expression (which may
           span many lines).
         - Comments: ; to end of line, as in the reader.

         LINE EDITING:
         - Real line editing matters for a dev tool.
           Raw SSH PTY + a line-editing layer.
         - Library choice deferred to build time:
           ocaml-linenoise is the leading candidate
           (small, Mirage-compatible-ish). Pure OCaml.
         - Supports: cursor movement, history (up/down),
           Ctrl-A/E, backspace, delete.
         - Search/history-reverse-search: nice-to-have,
           not v1-blocking.

         EVALUATION + PRINTING:
         - Evaluate the s-expression in the session env.
         - On success: pretty-print the resulting value.
           VList -> (a b c), VLambda -> #<lambda>,
           VRef -> #<ref event:uuid>, VInt -> 42, etc.
         - On error: print the error with location if
           possible (line/col from the reader), then a
           continuation prompt. Don't kill the session.

         META-COMMANDS (Lisp forms, not a separate
         command lexer — the reader handles them, the
         eval loop recognizes them as special forms):
           (define name expr)         bind + persist to store
           (edit name)                print htmx URL for AST editor
           (fork parent child)        create a branch
           (checkout branch)         switch session's branch
           (merge source target)      reviewer flow
           (describe 'type)           show type schema
           (list-bindings)           current env
           (load "file")              eval a file's contents
           (help)                     list specials
           Any other Lisp form        evaluate + print result

         SESSION STATE:
         - (define ...) persists to the store: survives
           image reboot (loaded on next boot of the
           branch).
         - REPL-local let bindings (e.g. (let ((x 5))
           ...) at top level): session-local, not
           persisted. Like bash variables.
         - Switching branch (checkout) within a session
           keeps REPL locals but changes which function
           defs are visible. (Decision: this is the
           'working directory' model — you carry your
           locals across branch switches.)
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection htmx-interface
   (doc "httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html, routes AS functions in the store")
   (implements image/lib/http.ml auth/lib/http.ml image/lib/session.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_ui_it11.py — system pages / /branches / /bindings / /binding/<name>")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_route_it9.py — (route ...) registers + dispatches user pages")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_types_it14.py — /types page + render-html tagged VStr dispatch")
    (smoke "GET user route still calls zero-arg handler (compat)")
    (smoke "POST user route hits same path handler; (request-method) => POST")
    (smoke "POST form-urlencoded body: (request-param \"title\") returns field")
    (smoke "GET query: (request-param \"q\") returns query value")
    (smoke "(redirect \"/todos\") => 303 Location: /todos")
    (smoke "POST /api/recipe/todo then POST /todos title=… creates row + redirects; GET /todos lists it"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The image's htmx endpoint serves two kinds of
         pages:

         1. SYSTEM pages (always present, part of the
            OCaml image):
            /health            health check
            /inspect/:id       browse any stored value
            /branches          list + fork/merge UI
            /types             type browser (type_defs)
            /data …            data rows browser (full CRUD;
                               see image.data-browser)
            /bindings          function browser (per branch)

         2. USER pages (defined as functions in the store):
            registered via (route "/path" fn-name).
            The route handler runs with a REQUEST CONTEXT
            (method, path, params) and returns a value the
            system renders (HTML, redirect, text, etc).

         Routes ARE functions in the store. Adding a route
         = (route "/foo" 'foo-handler). This makes the
         user-facing UI part of the live image, editable
         via the same mechanisms as everything else.

         ROUTE VISIBILITY (auth.route-visibility — planned):
         User routes default private (session required).
         (route path handler 'public) opts into anonymous GET.
         Kernel paths (/data /admin /api/eval /bindings /edit …)
         are hard-private in OCaml and cannot be opened by Lisp.
         Gate runs before handler dispatch (extend launch, do not
         invent a middleware framework).

         pure-html (the dream_html HTML generation core,
         usable WITHOUT Dream) is used for HTML generation
         (per poohstack convention; use Hx.* not raw
         strings at the OCaml layer).

         =========================================================
         USER-ROUTE HTTP METHODS (it33 — PLAN, not yet shipped)
         =========================================================

         GOAL: Lisp pages can accept browser forms. Todo and every
         other dogfood app need POST without leaving the image.

         METHODS on user routes (view_bindings paths):
         - GET  — already shipped (call handler, render value)
         - POST — REQUIRED this plan
         - PUT / PATCH / DELETE — deferred (not needed for form
           dogfood; may map later; do not invent now)

         MATCHING: exact path string only (status quo). No path
         params (/todos/:id) in this plan. Method does NOT select
         a different handler — same route path → same handler name
         for GET and POST. Handler branches on (request-method).

         SYSTEM routes keep exclusive claim on their paths:
         /api/*, /edit/*, /health, /bindings, … never fall through
         to user routes (status quo dispatch order).

         BODY (POST):
         - Read full body (reuse existing body reader in http.ml).
         - Content-Type application/x-www-form-urlencoded:
           parse into string key→value map (reuse form_field style
           decode; empty values allowed; last wins on dup keys).
         - Other content-types: raw body available via
           (request-body); params map empty unless also on query.
         - Size: soft cap 64 KiB for v1; oversize → 413 text error
           (do not eval handler).

         QUERY (GET and POST):
         - Parse target query string into the same params map.
         - Conflict rule: form field WINS over query key of same
           name (POST body is the intentional write path).

         =========================================================
         REQUEST CONTEXT (dynamic, handler-scoped)
         =========================================================

         Design choice: DYNAMIC BINDING, not a mandatory handler
         argument. Zero-arg handlers keep working:
           (define hi-page (lambda () (render-html "…")))
         Interactive handlers read request via prims while the
         HTTP dispatch is inside call_handler:

           (request-method)     -> VStr "GET" | "POST"
           (request-path)       -> VStr path without query
           (request-param k)    -> VStr value | VNil if missing
           (request-params)     -> VStr JSON object of all params
           (request-body)       -> VStr raw body ("" on GET)

         Implementation sketch (mirrors with-transaction scope):
         - Session.t gains mutable current_request : request option
         - type request = {
             method_ : [`GET|`POST];
             path : string;
             params : (string * string) list;  (* or Hashtbl *)
             body : string;
           }
         - http.ml builds request from meth + path + query + body
         - Session.call_handler ~request sets current_request,
           runs (handler-name) zero-arg apply, clears in finalize
         - Prims registered at session-make (image layer, like
           whoami) read Session.current_request; if None:
             (request-method) etc raise clean User_error
             "no active request" (REPL/eval outside HTTP)

         WHY NOT pass (lambda (req) …) as required arity:
         - Breaks every existing zero-arg page (hello, current todo)
         - Forces a VDict / record convention early
         - Dynamic prims match (whoami) / with-transaction style
         Optional later: also pass req dict as single arg if handler
         arity is 1 — NOT in this plan.

         =========================================================
         HANDLER RETURN VALUES
         =========================================================

         Keep existing render rules, add redirect:

         1. render-html tagged VStr (__html__:…)
            -> 200 text/html, body = raw HTML inside chrome
            (status quo; chrome wrap stays for full pages)
         2. bare VStr -> 200 text/html, <p> escaped text
         3. other values -> 200 text/html, <pre> pp_value
         4. NEW: (redirect path) -> tagged VStr __redirect__:path
            -> HTTP 303 See Other, Location: path, empty/minimal body
            Path must be absolute-on-host ("/todos") for v1;
            reject off-site (no "http://…") — User_error at prim
            or 500 if somehow returned.

         PRG: POST handlers that mutate SHOULD (redirect …) so
         refresh does not re-POST. Dogfood todo does this.

         Htmx partials: if request has header HX-Request: true,
         v1 MAY still wrap chrome (acceptable); optional later:
         skip chrome when HX-Request. Not required for form POST
         dogfood.

         Handler errors (Interp_error / missing handler):
         - GET: 500 HTML error page (status quo)
         - POST: 500 HTML error page (same); do not redirect

         =========================================================
         DOGFOOD: INTERACTIVE TODO (acceptance for this plan)
         =========================================================

         Upgrade Session.recipe_todo so /todos is a real form app:

         Type: todo-item {title, done}  (unchanged)

         Functions (illustrative; exact HTML may vary):
           todo/add     — (lambda (title) (create …))
           todo/list    — (lambda () (rows 'todo-item 50))
           todo/toggle  — flip done via update-field / get+dict-set
           todo/page    — method-aware page:
             POST: (todo/add (request-param "title"))
                   then (redirect "/todos")
             GET:  render-html with:
                   - <form method="POST" action="/todos">
                       <input name="title"> + submit
                   - list of items (title + done)
                   - optional small POST forms/buttons for toggle
                     (same path with action=toggle&id=… OR
                      separate route /todos/toggle — pick one in make;
                      prefer same-path + request-param "action")

         Route: (route "/todos" "todo/page") only is enough if
         action dispatch is param-based. Extra routes OK if clearer.

         Smoke path:
           POST /api/recipe/todo
           POST /todos  title=milk   -> 303 /todos
           GET  /todos               -> body contains milk
           Functions browser shows package todo

         =========================================================
         MAKE CHECKLIST (implementation order)
         =========================================================

         1. Session.request type + current_request field + accessors
         2. Register request-* and redirect prims (session-scoped)
         3. http.ml: parse query+form into request; allow POST on
            user-route fallthrough; call_handler ~request
         4. http.ml: dispatch redirect sentinel -> 303 Location
         5. Upgrade recipe_todo + home copy if needed
         6. Smoke script (e.g. /tmp/pp_post_routes_it33.py)
         7. Keep GET zero-arg hello recipe green

         OUT OF SCOPE THIS PLAN:
         - path parameters (/items/:id)
         - multipart file upload
         - JSON body auto-parse to dict prims
         - CSRF tokens (localhost/dev trust; file issue if prod needs)
         - (hx-get)/(hx-post) Lisp prims (still embed in HTML strings)
         - Solo5 / gluten-mirage HTTP
         - PUT/PATCH/DELETE user routes

         =========================================================
         ROUTE VISIBILITY (see auth.route-visibility — planned)
         =========================================================
         Authority for "may this request run this page?" is the
         OCaml launch gate, not Lisp inside the handler.

         - User routes default private (session or Basic required).
         - (route path handler 'public) → anonymous GET allowed.
         - System hard-private prefixes never become public via
           view_bindings (/admin /data /api/eval /bindings /edit …).
         - System hard-public remains /login /health /logout.
         - Optional auth: resolve identity even on public paths.
         - Anonymous: system/main, whoami anonymous, no writes v1.
         - Migration 006: view_bindings.visibility text default
           'private'. list_routes/register_route carry the flag.
         - /pages shows visibility badge.

         v1 SHIPPED (pre-it33):
         - httpaf-lwt-unix HTTP server
         - system pages listed above (except /inspect)
         - user routes via (route ...) + render-html (GET only)

         DEFERRED beyond it33:
         - /inspect/:id (see image.inspector)
         - (hx-get)/(hx-post) Lisp prims
         - Solo5 HTTP via gluten-mirage + httpaf
         - richer system-browser chrome (own subsection)
         - public-write routes / CSRF (route-visibility phase 2)
   |))
   )
  )

  (subsection http-robustness
   (doc "HTTP server survival under load + peer disconnect. The image must
         never exit because a client went away mid-response.")
   (implements image/lib/http.ml image/lib/http_core.ml image/lib/http_agent.ml)
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|Invariants 1,2,3,5 implemented 2026-07-17 after the
      pricklypear.rocks HN crash post mortem. Invariant 4 (narrow
      request_mutex) is DEFERRED to BORGE-1784301597: agent_say reads
      t.manifest.username live, so releasing the mutex across page_fn
      needs a per-request manifest/env snapshot refactor to avoid
      attributing agent messages to the wrong user in multi-tenant use.
      The inflight cap (invariant 5) was moved INSIDE launch rather than
      at the connection establisher so /health (which bypasses launch)
      is never blocked by page traffic — a connection-level gate wedged
      /health behind mutex-serialized page work in load testing.

      VERIFICATION (dev stack, 2026-07-17):
      - dune build image + dune runtest --force: 9 suites green
        incl. new image/test/http_robust_test.ml (3 tests: closed-writer
        predicate, safe-wrapper swallow, inflight acquire/release).
      - /tmp/pp_http_robust.py abort-storm: 64, 200, and 500 concurrent
        aborted requests (RST mid-response). image PID UNCHANGED across
        each storm; /health 200 mid-storm and after; 0 'Fatal error' in
        image.log. Pre-fix the same pattern reproduced the prod crash
        (Failure "cannot write to closed writer" -> process exit).
      - refresh_ui_shell warn spam: 1 line per 60s retry window (was
        hundreds per minute under load) — invariant 3 cache confirmed.
      NOT YET VERIFIED on prod (155.138.208.45); deploy + watchdog soak
      pending. Invariant 4 unblocks the throughput ceiling but is not a
      crash fix.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "/tmp/pp_http_robust.py — abort N concurrent requests mid-response; image stays up, /health 200 throughout")
    (smoke "open SSE /api/agent stream, disconnect client; no Fatal error in image.log, process pid unchanged")
    (smoke "fire M concurrent mixed routes (page + SSE + /api/eval); all complete or peer-gone, zero process exits")
    (test "image/test/http_robust_test.ml — closed-writer guard returns () not raises; catch-body does not re-write to dead writer"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         =========================================================
         MOTIVATION (production incident 2026-07-16)
         =========================================================
         pricklypear.rocks was shared on Hacker News. Under real
         traffic (humans clicking, reloading, aborting requests
         mid-response) the image process crashed repeatedly —
         ~15 watchdog restarts over ~26h, including a 4-crash
         burst in 5 minutes. Root cause was NOT load exhaustion:
         each crash was a single top-level uncaught exception
            Fatal error: exception
              Failure("cannot write to closed writer")
         which exits the single-image process. The watchdog (root
         cron, 1-minute granularity) restarted it each time, but
         the same aborted-request pattern killed it again.

         The httpaf body writer raises Failure("cannot write to
         closed writer") when you write to a response whose client
         has already disconnected. The agent SSE path was already
         hardened against this (is_closed_writer_exn /
         close_writer_quiet in http_agent.ml) but EVERY OTHER
         response path was unguarded: html_response, text_response,
         json_response, fragment_response, redirect_response, and
         the httpaf error_handler itself.

         The trap: launch's Lwt.catch error branch tried to render a
         500 page by calling html_response on the SAME dead writer
         the original handler failed on — so the recovery write
         threw the same Failure, now inside the catch handler where
         nothing guards it, and it propagated to top level.

         This subsection specifies the invariants that make a
         peer-disconnect a non-event, plus the concurrency model
         needed so the server does not serialize all traffic behind
         one lock (which would make it fall over on throughput
         even once crashes are fixed).

         =========================================================
         INVARIANT 1 — PEER-DISCONNECT IS NEVER FATAL
         =========================================================
         Every code path that writes an HTTP response body MUST
         tolerate a closed/gone writer by returning () (or a
         benign `Peer_gone signal), never by raising. A client
         closing its tab is the single most common event on a web
         server; it must not take the process down.

         Mechanism: generalize the existing SSE hardening up into
         http_core and apply it everywhere a response is written:

           - is_closed_writer_exn : exn -> bool
               (already in http_agent.ml; move to http_core, export)
           - safe_write : Body.t -> string -> unit
               write_string wrapped; swallows closed-writer Failure
           - safe_close : Body.t -> unit
               close_writer wrapped; swallows closed-writer Failure

         Wrap the body of EVERY response helper:
           html_response, text_response, json_response,
           fragment_response, redirect_response.
         These all currently call Httpaf.Reqd.respond_with_string
         or Body.write_string + close_writer directly; they must
         instead route through safe_write / safe_close (or catch
         the Failure around respond_with_string).

         The httpaf error_handler (http.ml error_handler) writes
         unguarded via Body.write_string + close_writer; it MUST
         be guarded the same way — it runs on connection-level
         errors where the peer is very often already gone.

         =========================================================
         INVARIANT 2 — ERROR RECOVERY MUST NOT RE-WRITE A DEAD WRITER
         =========================================================
         launch's Lwt.catch exception branch (http.ml) currently
         responds to a handler failure by calling html_response
         ... ~status:`Internal_server_error on the same reqd. If
         the original failure was a closed-writer (peer gone), the
         recovery write fails the same way and escapes the catch.

         Rule: the catch branch MUST detect the closed-writer case
         (is_closed_writer_exn on the caught exn) and, instead of
         rendering a 500 page, just safe_close the (already-dead)
         writer and return. Only when the writer is still alive do
         we render the 500 error page.

         Concretely: in the (fun exn -> ...) handler, branch:
           if is_closed_writer_exn exn -> safe_close; Lwt.return_unit
           else -> Ops_incidents emit + html_response 500 (as today)
         This breaks the throw-in-the-catch loop that was the
         actual crash mechanism.

         =========================================================
         INVARIANT 3 — ui/nav FAILURE IS NOT PER-REQUEST NOISE
         =========================================================
         refresh_ui_shell runs on every request (http.ml launch).
         When ui/nav is broken on the branch it logs a [warn]
         line PER REQUEST — under HN traffic this produced
         hundreds of warn lines and wasted a Lisp eval per page.

         Rule: refresh_ui_shell MUST cache its failure state per
         branch (at least short-TTL in-memory): once ui/nav has
         failed on a branch, do not re-attempt and re-warn on every
         request; downgrade to a single debug line (or a periodic
         retry on the order of minutes, not per-request). The
         emergency chrome fallback already serves the page; the
         spam is pure waste. This is not a crash fix but a
         log-hygiene + cost fix surfaced by the same incident.

         =========================================================
         INVARIANT 4 — CONCURRENCY: IDENTITY ATTACH IS SERIALIZED,
         PAGE WORK IS NOT
         =========================================================
         Status quo: a single global request_mutex
         (http_core.ml:223) is held for the ENTIRE request lifecycle
         — identity resolve, classify, refresh_ui_shell, AND
         page_fn (including slow LLM/SSE streaming). This serializes
         the whole site to one-request-at-a-time and, worse, lets
         one streaming agent chat block /login and / for everyone.

         Rule: request_mutex protects ONLY the identity-attach
         critical section (resolve_identity + attach_from_user /
         attach_anonymous + the classify that depends on the
         attached branch). The mutex is released BEFORE page_fn
         runs. Page work — including user-route handlers, /api/eval,
         and the agent SSE streaming loop — runs WITHOUT holding
         the global lock.

         Rationale: the mutex exists to prevent the Alice/Bob race
         on session env / branch context during the auth gate. It
         does not need to protect page rendering, which is
         request-local. Two concurrent page_fns on the same
         session is acceptable because (a) Lisp route handlers are
         pure w.r.t. the request context (current_request is
         dynamic, handler-scoped) and (b) DB prims use their own
         Store connections / transactions. If a future shared
         mutable session field needs serialization, give THAT
         field its own narrower lock — do not re-globalize.

         =========================================================
         INVARIANT 5 — ACCEPT-SIDE CONNECTION CAP
         =========================================================
         Status quo: serve (http.ml) calls
         Httpaf_lwt_unix.Server.create_connection_handler with no
         limit and Lwt_io.establish_server_with_client_socket with
         no backlog/fd cap. Under a spike the accept loop can
         exhaust fds or memory even after crashes are fixed.

         Rule: gate the acceptor on a bounded semaphore (an
         Lwt_semaphore or a counter with a max, default 256
         in-flight requests, configurable via PP_HTTP_MAX_INFLIGHT).
         Requests beyond the cap are rejected early with a 503
         (or simply not accepted until a slot frees). This bounds
         fd/memory usage to a known ceiling regardless of traffic.
         The cap is a safety valve, not a throughput target —
         normal load should be well under it.

         NOTE: this is defense-in-depth on top of the crash fix.
         The crash fix (invariants 1-3) is what stops the death
         loop; the cap (invariant 5) plus the mutex narrowing
         (invariant 4) are what make the server survive the next
         traffic spike on throughput, not just on not-crashing.

         =========================================================
         WHAT THIS IS NOT
         =========================================================
         - Not a general middleware framework. The launch gate
           stays; we just narrow the lock scope.
         - Not a PG connection pool. The single Store conn remains
           v1; throughput of DB prims is bounded by it, but that
           is acceptable for v1 dogfood load. A pool is a separate
           later iteration if measured needed.
         - Not async LLM token streaming over the wire. The SSE
           path stays progress events; this work just makes it
           not hold the global mutex and not crash on disconnect.
         - Does not change route visibility / auth gate semantics.
           The gate still runs before page_fn; only the lock scope
           around it changes.

         =========================================================
         MAKE CHECKLIST (implementation order)
         =========================================================
         1. http_core.ml: add is_closed_writer_exn, safe_write,
            safe_close (promote from http_agent.ml; keep the SSE
            copies as thin aliases or refactor to shared).
         2. Wrap html_response, text_response, json_response,
            fragment_response, redirect_response body writes with
            safe_write/safe_close (or catch respond_with_string).
         3. http.ml error_handler: guard Body.write_string +
            close_writer with safe_*.
         4. http.ml launch Lwt.catch branch: if is_closed_writer_exn
            on the caught exn, safe_close + return; else 500 as
            today. Do NOT render 500 onto a dead writer.
         5. Session.refresh_ui_shell: cache failure per branch
            (short TTL or until branch reload); downgrade repeat
            warns to debug. Emergency chrome fallback unchanged.
         6. http_core.ml: narrow request_mutex scope in launch to
            identity-attach + classify only; release before page_fn.
            Add a per-page-result lock only if a real shared-mutable
            need appears (it should not).
         7. http.ml serve: add Lwt_semaphore of PP_HTTP_MAX_INFLIGHT
            (default 256) around the acceptor / request_handler;
            reject overflow with 503.
         8. Pull agent SSE streaming drain (http_agent.ml api_agent
            streaming branch) out from under request_mutex — it
            must run in the page-work phase, not the attach phase.
         9. Smoke scripts: /tmp/pp_http_robust.py (abort N concurrent
            mid-response; assert pid unchanged + /health 200),
            /tmp/pp_sse_disconnect.py (open stream, drop client,
            assert no Fatal error in log).
         10. Keep all existing smoke tests green (route, eval, ui,
             types, post routes, e2e).

         =========================================================
         VERIFICATION BARS
         =========================================================
         - dune build image && dune runtest (image) green.
         - /tmp/pp_http_robust.py: fire 64 concurrent requests that
           abort at 50ms; after, image pid is UNCHANGED and
           /health returns 200. (Pre-fix this reproduces the crash.)
         - /tmp/pp_sse_disconnect.py: open /api/agent stream,
           close the socket after 1s; grep image.log for 'Fatal
           error' over the next 10s -> zero matches.
         - Watchdog log over a 1h soak under mixed traffic: zero
           'pricklypear down' lines.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection primitives-layer
   (doc "image/lib/primitives.ml: the ~20 impure fns registered at boot")
   (implements image/lib/primitives.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image && cd lisp && dune build && dune runtest")
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml 48 alcotest cases; lisp/test/eval_test.ml 30 pure tests"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         See lisp-interpreter.primitives for the full
         primitive list. This subsection covers the IMAGE
         layer's responsibility: registering them with
         the interpreter at boot.

         The interpreter is pure and knows nothing about
         Postgres, htmx, or SSH. The IMAGE layer:
         1. Opens a Postgres connection (v1: pgx_lwt +
            custom Io_unix shim; Solo5 later may use
            pgx_lwt_mirage behind the same Store.t).
         2. Constructs an initial env with VPrim values
            bound to names like 'get', 'create', etc.
         3. The interpreter's Prim call dispatches by name
            to the OCaml function the image registered.
         4. Each primitive handles its own transaction
            (SERIALIZABLE by default) and permission
            checks (per multi-user.permissions; author-
            scoped filtering still partial).

         This separation is what makes the interpreter
         testable without any services running (pure
         lisp/test/eval_test.ml, no DB, no HTTP).

         v1 SHIPPED registration path: real.
         DEFERRED: full prim table completeness (see
         lisp-interpreter.primitives); Solo5 pgx path.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection system-browser
   (doc "The live environment UI: browse branches, functions, types as a place you live")
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET / shows user+branch chrome and links to about/functions/repl")
    (smoke "product nav comes from (ui/nav) when shell loaded; not silent OCaml product twin")
    (smoke "product CSS comes from (ui/css) / ui/theme when shell loaded; not silent full OCaml design-system twin")
    (smoke "nav has no /edit/hello; Pages links to /pages")
    (smoke "GET /pages columns: page URL + handler function")
    (smoke "GET /page/hello shows route + handler + callee list")
    (smoke "GET /bindings lists packages including (kernel) and (root)/workspace pkgs")
    (smoke "GET /bindings?pkg=(kernel) lists primitives e.g. create, +")
    (smoke "GET /bindings?pkg=(kernel)&fn=create shows read-only prim docs")
    (smoke "GET /bindings?pkg=todo lists todo/* functions")
    (smoke "GET /bindings?pkg=todo&fn=list shows function detail + edit link")
    (smoke "GET /bindings?q=todo filters; mobile-width usable (nav wraps)")
    (smoke "define todo/list + call (todo/list); used-by shows /todos when routed"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         VOCABULARY (must not conflate):
         - Page / endpoint = route URL (e.g. /hello)
         - Function = named binding (e.g. hi-page or todo/list)
         - Package = in-branch name prefix (todo/), NOT a branch
         - Branch = versioned code world (system/main)
         A page RUNS a function; you EDIT functions, OPEN pages.
         Packages ORGANIZE functions inside one branch.

         v1 SHIPPED:
         - Chrome: whoami, branch, uptime, nav
         - Home entry points + AI propose form
         - /about concepts guide
         - /pages list + /page/<path> inspector
         - Flat /bindings table (name, effects, author, edit, inspect)

         FUNCTIONS BROWSER (/bindings) — Smalltalk-ish drilldown:

         PHILOSOPHY:
         - Majority of programs are Lisp bindings in function_defs.
         - Kernel primitives are OCaml (performant, trusted); still
           BROWSABLE so the system is legible, but read-only.
         - Optional future: mark some OCaml modules as "image libs"
           for hot paths; not required for this cut.

         MOBILE FIRST (three-step drilldown, not three columns):
         URL state via query params (works on phones):
           Step 1  GET /bindings
                   → list packages only (big tap targets)
                   Always includes virtual package "(kernel)"
                   Workspace packages from prefix grouping + "(root)"
           Step 2  GET /bindings?pkg=todo
                   → list functions in that package
                   Kernel: list prim names in catalog
           Step 3  GET /bindings?pkg=todo&fn=list
                   → detail: source/docs, effects, author, used-by,
                     edit/inspect links (workspace) OR read-only
                     kernel doc (primitives)
         Desktop MAY show a wider table when no pkg selected
         (grouped overview), but pkg/fn drilldown is the canonical
         model and must work at ~390px width.
         Back links: Functions → package → (back to packages).

         KERNEL CATALOG:
         - Virtual package name: "(kernel)" (not a real binding prefix)
         - Source: prim registry + short docs (name, summary, effects)
         - Kind badge: primitive | function
         - No Save on primitives; link to About/docs if needed

         WORKSPACE (existing + keep):
         - Group by package_of_name; search q=; author filter
         - used-by pages; create package+local form
         - Edit only for function_defs rows

         UI SHELL AS LISP (authoritative — 2026-07-12):

         RULE: If a human thinks of it as "the environment UI"
         (navbar, home workspace, app menus), it is Lisp on a
         branch — not a parallel OCaml product skin.

         - Product shell bindings (required when seed runs):
             ui/menu   — menu tree (prefer zero-arg lambda that
                         returns a list; eager lists are fragile
                         if they call menu/* at define-time)
             ui/nav    — (lambda () (render-html (menu/bar …)))
             ui/home   — (lambda () …) home body HTML
             ui/theme  — css/sheet data (tokens + rules); Lisp,
                         not a CSS string blob
             ui/css    — (lambda () (css/render ui/theme))
                         adapter only; session caches its string
         - libs/ui/lib.pp seeds system/main (load-library "ui" /
           seed_stdlib). Personal may pin overrides; inherit main
           when no pin. Prefer thunks over eager value pins.
         - Chrome nav uses (ui/nav); GET / uses (ui/home);
           full-page <style> uses rendered (ui/css) — see
           image.product-theme.
         - Identity strip only (brand, username, branch, uptime)
           stays OCaml — not product structure.
         - Kernel OCaml pages stay OCaml until deliberately moved:
           /login, /health, auth middleware, /bindings,/branches,
           /edit, /inspect, emergency recovery. These are host UI,
           not a second apps menu.
         - EMERGENCY chrome (nav/home/theme fail or unbound):
           minimal, labeled failure (must not look like a healthy
           product menu or full design system). Silent rich OCaml
           default_nav / default_home / full inline CSS twin is
           REJECTED architecture — debt to remove.
         - Rehydrate load order: parents first, child overrides
           last (so personal ui/menu can call system menu/link;
           personal theme/* tokens can override system tokens).
         - Edit shell via Functions → package ui (mobile drilldown),
           including ui/theme and theme/* tokens.

         LAYERING SUMMARY:
           Lisp  = the computer you live in (shell + theme + apps)
           OCaml = host + gate (eval, store, auth, health, emergency)

         DEFERRED:
         - Remove silent default_nav_nodes / default_home_content
           and shrink html_response style blob to emergency_css
           (image.product-theme make)
         - ui/menu seed always lambda (load-order safe by default)
         - Move softer system pages (/about copy) into Lisp packages
         - Branch switcher; fork/merge UI
         - True three-column desktop layout (optional later)
         - Import/require, private symbols, rename-with-rewrite
         - Authoring new prims from the UI
         - Dedicated /theme form UI (Functions edit is enough v1)

         DATA BROWSER: full CRUD over data_rows is specified in
         image.data-browser (OCaml system pages, not a Lisp package).
         /types remains the type_defs index and links into /data/<type>.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection data-browser
   (doc "Generic OCaml system UI for full CRUD over shared data_rows by type")
   (status planned)
   (implements image/lib/http.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/store.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET /data lists types with row counts; shared-data banner present")
    (smoke "GET /data/<type> lists rows (default limit 50) with declared columns")
    (smoke "GET /data/<type>?offset=50 pages next slice")
    (smoke "GET /data/<type>/new shows create form from type_defs fields")
    (smoke "POST /data/<type> creates row from form fields; 303 to detail")
    (smoke "GET /data/<type>/<id> shows fields + edit form + delete")
    (smoke "POST /data/<type>/<id> updates fields; 303/reload detail")
    (smoke "POST /data/<type>/<id>/delete deletes row; 303 to type list")
    (smoke "GET /types links each type to /data/<type>")
    (smoke "GET /inspect/row/<type>/<id> redirects or aliases to /data/<type>/<id>")
    (smoke "type with no type_defs fields still allows raw JSON create/edit")
    (smoke "extra jsonb keys not in type_defs still shown on detail"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHY:
         /types shows declared shapes; /inspect/row dumps one row as
         pretty JSON. Neither is a place to live in the object store.
         Product packages (todo, notes, bible) have purpose-built UIs,
         but agents and humans still need a generic browser when there
         is no package UI, the package is broken, or the type is ad-hoc.

         LAYERING (locked):
         - OCaml system pages in image/lib/http.ml (+ thin helpers in
           crud.ml / store.ml as needed). Host/gate tier — same as
           /types, /bindings, /inspect, /edit.
         - NOT a Lisp package. Product shell stays Lisp; this is
           emergency/generic substrate UI that must work with empty
           or broken gardens.
         - Mutations call existing Crud/Store SQL paths. Do not invent
           a second data access layer.
         - data_rows are SHARED across branches (no branch_id). UI
           must say so on every list/detail surface.

         =========================================================
         ROUTES (full CRUD)
         =========================================================

         Index (Read types + counts):
           GET  /data
             - Table: type_name | declared fields | row count | link
             - Types = visible type_defs on current branch (Store.list_types)
             - Also surface type_names that have data_rows but no
               type_def (orphan data) so nothing is invisible
             - Banner: "Data rows are shared across branches"
             - Link back/forward with /types (types = schema index;
               /data = instances)

         List (Read many):
           GET  /data/<type>
             - Query: limit (default 50, max 200), offset (default 0)
             - Columns: id (short) + each declared field (truncated)
               + updated_at if cheap; link id → detail
             - Pagination: Prev / Next when more rows exist
             - Actions: [New row] → /data/<type>/new
             - Empty state: no rows yet + create CTA
             - Order: created_at ASC (match Crud.list) unless a later
               plan picks something smarter

         Create:
           GET  /data/<type>/new
             - Form: one input per declared field (text inputs v1)
             - If type has ZERO declared fields OR user wants freeform:
               textarea name=fields_json for raw JSON object
             - POST target: /data/<type>
           POST /data/<type>
             - Content-Type application/x-www-form-urlencoded
             - Build fields jsonb from declared field names present
               in the form; if fields_json posted and non-empty, that
               wins (power path)
             - author = Session username
             - On success: 303 Location: /data/<type>/<id>
             - On failure: 400 with error card + back link

         Detail / Update / Delete:
           GET  /data/<type>/<id>
             - Show all fields (declared first, then extra jsonb keys
               not in type_defs — do not hide unknowns)
             - Edit form prefilled (same field rules as create)
             - Delete form POST /data/<type>/<id>/delete (confirm via
               button label; no JS required)
             - Meta: id, type, author, created_at, updated_at when
               available
             - Links: back to type list, /types, REPL hint
           POST /data/<type>/<id>
             - Update fields (full replace of fields jsonb from form,
               same merge rules as create). Uses Crud.update path.
             - 303 back to detail on success
           POST /data/<type>/<id>/delete
             - Crud.delete; 303 to /data/<type>
             - Missing row: 404 card, not silent success

         URL parsing notes:
         - type_name may contain only safe path segments in v1
           (letters, digits, hyphen, underscore). Reject or 400
           weird paths; no path-param injection into SQL (always
           parameterized — already true in Crud).
         - id is UUID string.
         - Prefer longest-match dispatch:
             /data/<type>/new
             /data/<type>/<id>/delete
             /data/<type>/<id>
             /data/<type>
             /data
           so "new" is never treated as an id.

         =========================================================
         FIELD / JSON RULES (v1)
         =========================================================
         - type_defs.fields is a JSON array of field-name strings
           (no field types yet — object-model.type-system deferred).
         - Form values are strings; store as JSON strings in the
           fields object. Do NOT guess number/bool coercion in v1
           unless the existing value is already that shape and a
           later polish wants round-trip fidelity.
         - Raw JSON textarea: must parse as a JSON object; reject
           arrays/scalars with a clear error.
         - Extra keys on existing rows: display on detail; include
           in edit form as additional inputs OR preserve via hidden
           merge so update does not drop them. REQUIRED: updates
           must not silently strip unknown keys.

         =========================================================
         RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING PAGES
         =========================================================
         - /types: keep as type_defs browser; each row links to
           /data/<type>. Optional: add row count column on /types
           once count helper exists (nice-to-have, not blocking).
         - /inspect/row/<type>/<id>: redirect (302/303) to
           /data/<type>/<id> OR thin alias rendering the same page.
           Prefer redirect so one canonical URL.
         - Product apps unchanged: /todos, /notes, /bible stay Lisp.
         - Nav: emergency/system links may include Data → /data;
           product ui/menu MAY add Data later (Lisp choice, not
           required for make).

         =========================================================
         STORE / CRUD HELPERS (implementation discretion)
         =========================================================
         Add only what the UI needs if missing:
         - count rows by type_name (for index)
         - list type_names present in data_rows (orphans)
         - list rows with limit+offset (Crud.list today is limit-only;
           extend with offset or a store helper)
         - optional: fetch author/created_at/updated_at for detail
         Keep Lisp prim surface stable unless a prim gap blocks the
         browser (prefer OCaml-only helpers for offset/count).

         =========================================================
         AUTH / SAFETY
         =========================================================
         - Same session gate as other authenticated system pages.
         - Not admin-only in v1 (any logged-in user can browse/mutate
           shared data — matches Lisp CRUD prims today).
         - No CSRF token infrastructure in v1 (status quo for other
           POSTs); cookie session + same-site assumptions hold.
         - Never interpolate type/id into SQL strings.

         =========================================================
         NON-GOALS (v1)
         =========================================================
         - Field type system / validation beyond JSON object shape
         - Full-text search UI (find-text exists for Lisp; wire later)
         - Inline htmx row edit without full page (optional polish)
         - Bulk delete / CSV import-export
         - Per-row branch scoping (data is shared by design)
         - Lisp-redefinable browser UI
         - SPA / client-side routing

         =========================================================
         ACCEPTANCE NARRATIVE
         =========================================================
         Human can: open /data → pick a type → page through rows →
         open a row → edit a field → save → create a new row →
         delete a row → land back on the type list. All without
         REPL. Agent can use the same URLs or keep using CRUD prims.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection product-theme
   (doc "Product presentation as Lisp data: css/* sheet compiler, ui/theme + ui/css, emergency CSS only in OCaml")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "(css/render (css/sheet (body margin 0 background \"#d4d0c8\"))) contains body{ and margin:0")
    (smoke "(function-source \"ui/theme\") or (function-source \"ui/css\") non-empty after seed")
    (smoke "(export-package \"ui\") contains define ui/theme or define ui/css")
    (smoke "GET / authenticated response <style> matches rendered (ui/css) distinctive marker, not only emergency")
    (smoke "personal (define theme/bg …) or ui/theme pin changes next full-page CSS; checkout main restores")
    (smoke "(define ui/css (lambda () (error \"boom\"))) still serves page with emergency CSS + labeled failure")
    (smoke "htmx fragment / partial responses do not re-inject full stylesheet")
    (smoke "login page stays pre-session OCaml mini stylesheet (no Lisp theme required)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         WHY:
         Today almost all product CSS is a giant inline string in
         image/lib/http.ml html_response — inaccessible from the
         garden, unversioned as Lisp, dual to the product-shell
         rule that already moved nav/home into libs/ui. Widget
         packages emit class names that only work because that
         OCaml blob knows them. That is a black box.

         DECISION (Path B — honest Lisp):
         Presentation is Lisp data, same substrate as shell and
         apps. A small css/* compiler (sibling of html/* in std)
         turns sheets into CSS text. ui/theme holds the sheet;
         ui/css is a thin (lambda () (css/render ui/theme))
         adapter for session refresh. Humans and agents edit
         sexps / tokens via Functions — not escaped CSS inside
         a lambda, and not a second language document as the
         native store.

         REJECTED:
         - Path hybrid: (define ui/css (lambda () "body{…}"))
           as the primary form (CSS-in-string wearing Lisp hat)
         - Path A as native store: bare CSS document / data_rows
           stylesheet type as the only theme representation
           (allowed later as import/export sugar, not authority)
         - Static /assets/*.css as the product theme path
         - Silent full OCaml design-system twin beside Lisp theme
         - Per-request uncached re-eval of ui/css without shell
           refresh discipline (mirror ui/nav caching lessons)

         =========================================================
         css/*  (libs/std/lib.pp — seed with std, before ui)
         =========================================================
         Parallel to html/*: structure in Lisp, render to string.

         Core bindings (v1 — keep small):
           (css/sheet rule…)     → sheet value (list-tagged data)
           (css/use other)       → splice/concat another sheet
           (css/raw "…")         → escape hatch for rare CSS
           (css/render sheet)    → CSS string

         Rule encoding (selectors as strings; declarations data):
           - (selector prop val prop val…)
             selector may be a symbol (body, header) or string
             (".card", "nav a", "details.ol-node > .ol-sum")
           - ((sel1 sel2 …) prop val …)
             multi-selector group → "sel1, sel2 { … }"
           - ((media "max-width:40rem") rule…)
             nested @media block
           - Values: number | string | symbol (symbol resolves
             as token binding if bound, else as CSS ident)
           - Do NOT invent a full selector AST or px-type system
             in v1. Selectors stay strings/symbols; props are
             alternating key/value data.

         Emit rules (css/render):
           - prop symbols/strings → CSS property names
             (margin → margin; font-family → font-family;
              accept underscore→hyphen if needed)
           - numbers: print without quotes (0, 1.4)
           - strings: as CSS values (caller includes units)
           - stable, readable output; order preserved
           - css/raw segments pass through unchanged

         Composition:
           (css/sheet
             (css/use ui/theme-base)
             (css/use todo/theme)    ; optional later
             (body background theme/bg))

         html/* analogy docstring in std:
           "sheets → css/render; no raw rule soup in apps"
           (css/raw remains the deliberate guest).

         =========================================================
         ui/theme + tokens + ui/css  (libs/ui/lib.pp)
         =========================================================
         Required product-shell bindings (with ui/menu ui/nav ui/home):

           theme/* tokens (examples; exact set is implementation
           discretion so long as the classic-Mac look ports):
             theme/bg theme/ink theme/panel theme/chrome
             theme/link theme/err theme/ok …

           ui/theme
             A css/sheet (or zero-arg lambda returning a sheet)
             expressing the full product design system currently
             hard-coded in http.ml (body, header, nav, cards,
             tables, forms, outline, todos, calendar, bible,
             weather class contracts, mobile breakpoints).
             Prefer data + theme/* tokens over magic hex literals
             so personal gardens can (define theme/bg …).

           ui/css
             (lambda () (css/render ui/theme))
             or (lambda () (css/render (ui/theme))) if theme is
             a thunk. Adapter only — not where humans paste CSS.

         Class contract:
           - Shared classes used by multiple packages live in
             ui/theme (todo-box, ol-node, cal-grid, card, …).
           - Package-local extras may keep a small (html/tag
             "style" …) or later (todo/theme) composed via
             css/use — weather/style today is acceptable local.
           - Moving the monolith out of OCaml does not require
             splitting packages on day one.

         Seed: seed_stdlib still load-library std then weather
         then ui … — css/* must exist before ui/theme renders.
         (std before ui already holds.)

         =========================================================
         Session cache + HTTP inject
         =========================================================
         Mirror ui/nav:

           Session.t gains mutable ui_css : string option
             Some s → product stylesheet text
             None   → use emergency_css

           refresh_ui_shell (rename meaning: refresh shell):
             existing (ui/nav) path unchanged
             plus eval (ui/css) → string | raw-html-as-text →
             cache; on miss/throw → ui_css <- None

           Invalidate / refresh when:
             define/load of ui/css, ui/theme, ui/nav, ui/home
             (and ideally theme/* token defines — at minimum any
             define that refresh_ui_shell already re-runs after
             shell names; token-only defines may require explicit
             ui/css refresh or re-eval on next request launch)
             checkout / branch switch (no stale other-branch CSS)
             load-library that may redefine shell

           html_response gains ~css parameter (or reads session):
             HTML.style [] "%s" css
             Authenticated full pages pass Session.ui_css |
             emergency_css.

           fragment_response / HX partials:
             do NOT re-inject the full stylesheet (page already
             has it). Same spirit as chrome-skip for fragments.

           login_form_page (pre-session):
             keeps a tiny OCaml-only mini stylesheet. Must not
             require Lisp theme. Not the product design system.

         =========================================================
         Emergency CSS (OCaml kernel — labeled, minimal)
         =========================================================
         emergency_css is host recovery, not a product twin:
           - monospace / high-contrast / readable
           - styles emergency-banner, emergency-nav, basic links
           - MUST look broken or sparse on purpose if product
             theme fails (parallel emergency-nav rule)
           - After make: the 200-line classic-Mac blob MUST leave
             html_response; only emergency_css (+ login mini)
             remain as OCaml string stylesheets.

         Optional: when falling back, surface a one-line labeled
         banner in chrome ("product theme unbound — emergency css")
         consistent with emergency-nav labeling.

         =========================================================
         Authoring / multi-user / sync
         =========================================================
         - Edit via Functions → package ui → ui/theme | theme/*
           (same mobile drilldown as other shell bindings).
         - Personal pin: (define theme/bg "#e8f0e8") or full
           ui/theme override on user/personal; inherits main
           when no pin.
         - (export-package "ui") includes theme tokens + ui/theme
           + ui/css; pp_sync pull/push works unchanged.
         - Agents: pp_eval define/function-source — no new REST.
         - v1 non-goals: /theme textarea page, CSS file import
           pipeline, Tailwind/Sass build step, data_rows theme
           type as authority, per-user CSS without branch pins.

         =========================================================
         MAKE ORDER
         =========================================================
         1. css/* in libs/std + unit/smoke sexp→CSS
         2. Transcribe http.ml product blob → ui/theme (+ tokens)
            in libs/ui; define ui/css adapter
         3. Session ui_css + refresh_ui_shell extension
         4. html_response ~css inject from session
         5. Shrink OCaml to emergency_css (+ login mini)
         6. Smokes: seed marker, personal override, failure
            fallback, fragment no-reinject
         7. borge drift / resolve BORGE-1783837363

         STATUS: planned until make lands and silent full OCaml
         product CSS twin is gone.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection packages
   (doc "In-branch prefix packages for organizing functions (not branch ownership)")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build lisp image")
    (smoke "reader accepts symbol todo/list as Var")
    (smoke "(define todo/list (lambda () 1)) then (todo/list) => 1")
    (smoke "todo/list and calendar/list coexist on same branch")
    (smoke "/bindings groups todo/list under package todo")
    (smoke "(load-string \"(define pack/x (lambda () 7))\") then (pack/x) => 7")
    (smoke "(load-library \"todo\") installs /todos and todo/page")
    (smoke "libs/todo/lib.pp is source of truth; no OCaml string recipe body")
    (smoke "(load-library \"bible\") defines bible/verse bible/chapter bible/page + /bible")
    (smoke "after seed: (bible/verse \"John\" 3 16) returns BSB text containing God/world")
    (smoke "GET /bible?book=John&chapter=3 lists verse text")
    (smoke "GET /bible?ref=BSB:John:3:16 focuses that verse")
    (smoke "seed script idempotent: second run does not duplicate refs")
    (smoke "(function-source \"ui/nav\") returns non-empty lambda/list source")
    (smoke "(export-package \"ui\") contains define ui/nav")
    (smoke "(function-source \"ui/css\") or ui/theme non-empty after seed; export-package ui includes theme")
    (smoke "scripts/pp_sync.py status ui exits 0 with env set"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: flat binding names collide once many tools live on
         one branch (todo list vs calendar list).

         MODEL (v1):
         - Package is a PREFIX of a function name, separator `/`.
         - Full binding key remains (branch_id, name) with name the
           full string "todo/list". No packages table required.
         - package_of_name "todo/list" = "todo"; local = "list".
         - package_of_name "todo/ui/row" = "todo/ui"; local = "row".
         - package_of_name "hi-page" = "" (root).
         - Call and define use the full name: (todo/list), (define todo/list …).
         - Pages still route to full handler names:
           (route "/todos" "todo/list").

         ORTHOGONAL TO BRANCHES:
         - Branch = version + ownership (multi-user.namespaces).
         - Package = organization inside one branch.
         - UI must never call a branch a "package" or vice versa.

         NOT IN V1:
         - (require 'todo) / import aliases
         - Private package symbols
         - Automatic rename of call sites
         - Cross-branch qualified names (system/main:greet) — still
           multi-user.namespaces deferred item
         - Privileged global package registry

         AI / EDITOR:
         - Prefer namespaced names for new tools (todo/list not list).
         - AI propose form: optional package field + local name, or
           single name field that may contain /.
         - Function editor URL remains /edit/<full-name> with / in
           the path segment (encode if needed) or query name=;
           implementation may use /edit?name=todo/list if path
           routing is awkward — pick one and document in agent note.

         LIBRARY LOADER (implemented):
         Same Lisp source text in git repos and in the live system.
         OCaml provides only a pure loader (no app string blobs).

         On-disk layout (shipped in release tarball as libs/):
           libs/std/lib.pp     — list/*, html/*, css/*, menu/*, data/*
           libs/todo/lib.pp    — deftype + todo/* + routes + partials
           libs/hello/lib.pp   — optional hi-page recipe as Lisp
           libs/bible/lib.pp   — bible/* API + /bible (canon is data_rows)
           libs/ui/lib.pp      — ui/menu ui/nav ui/home ui/theme ui/css
           libs/ai/lib.pp      — ai/ask ai/do wrappers
           libs/chat/lib.pp    — multi-user lobby+DM (multi-user.chat)
           libs/plan/lib.pp    — Unix .plan status (multi-user.user-plan)

         Loader API:
           (load-string SRC)     read_all forms; eval_one each (define
                                 persists via existing intercept; route/
                                 deftype via prims). Stop on first error.
           (load-library NAME)   load file libs/<NAME>/lib.pp relative to
                                 PP_LIBS_DIR or ./libs (release CWD).

         Boot / recipes:
           seed_stdlib → load-library "std" then "ui" then "ai"
             then (when present) "chat" and "plan" for multi-user social
           recipe_todo → load-library "todo"
           recipe_hello → load-library "hello"
           recipe_bible → load-library "bible"  (types+fns+route ONLY;
                            does NOT bulk-insert verses)

         Release build MUST copy libs/ into the tarball.

         =========================================================
         LIBRARY SYNC CLI (2026-07-11 — implement)
         =========================================================
         PROBLEM: live function_defs drift from git libs/*.pp when users
         or AI edit in the browser. Push of kernel does NOT overwrite DB.
         Developers need explicit sync so laptop git matches language state.

         MODEL:
         - Local truth for recipes: libs/<pkg>/lib.pp
         - Live truth for running language: function_defs on current branch
         - CLI (scripts/pp_sync.py) uses authenticated /api/eval (pp_eval)
           and export prims — no direct Postgres from laptop required.

         EXPORT PRIMS (image):
           (function-source "ui/menu")
             → VStr pretty body only (lambda/list/…) for define
           (export-package "ui")
             → VStr multi-form lib.pp text:
               ;; package ui — exported from live branch
               (define ui/menu …)
               (define ui/nav …)
               … sorted by name
             Root package "" / "(root)" exports unprefixed names only.
             Kernel prims are never exported.
           (package-bindings "ui")
             → VList of VStr full names in that package on this branch

         CLI:
           scripts/pp_sync.py status [pkg]
             Compare libs/<pkg>/lib.pp define names vs live package-bindings.
             Report only-local / only-live / both (optional body hash later).
           scripts/pp_sync.py pull <pkg>
             Write libs/<pkg>/lib.pp from (export-package pkg).
             Creates directory if missing. Does not auto-commit git.
           scripts/pp_sync.py push <pkg>
             (load-library pkg) via pp_eval — file → live (upsert defines).
           scripts/pp_sync.py pull-all
             Pull every non-empty package that exists live or under libs/.

         ENV: same as pp_eval (PP_URL PP_USER PP_PASS, ~/.config/pricklypear/env).

         OUT OF SCOPE v1:
           - data_rows sync (todos/bible text)
           - automatic merge conflict resolution
           - function revision history table
           - Doltgres

         VERIFY:
           - (export-package "ui") contains define ui/nav
           - pull ui rewrites libs/ui/lib.pp; status shows both
           - push ui after edit reloads live

         =========================================================
         BIBLE LIBRARY (BSB dogfood — PLAN locked 2026-07-11)
         =========================================================

         GOAL: read BSB from the live image via Lisp + /bible page.
         Canon lives in Postgres data_rows (shared across branches).
         Functions live in package bible/ via load-library.

         DECISIONS:
         - Translation v1: BSB only (attribution in page footer / lib header).
         - Storage: data_rows, NOT giant define / NOT .sexp corpus at runtime.
         - Ref string (human + share + seed key):
             BSB:<Abbrev>:<chapter>:<verse>
             e.g. BSB:John:3:16
                  BSB:1Cor:13:4
                  BSB:Ps:23:1
         - Book token in ref = stable ABBREV (no spaces).
           Display name on bible-book rows separately.
         - data_rows.id stays UUID PK (migration 003). Ref is NOT the PK.
           fields.ref holds BSB:Abbrev:C:V uniquely.
         - Optional seed optimization: deterministic UUID v5 from ref so
           tooling can recompute id; Lisp users still call bible/* not raw
           get-by-uuid. Either UUID-default or v5 is fine if ref unique.

         TYPES (deftype from libs/bible/lib.pp):
           bible-book:
             name, abbrev, ordinal, testament
             (~66 rows; menus / prev-next / name→abbrev map)
           bible-verse:
             translation, book, abbrev, chapter, verse, text, ref
             chapter/verse as JSON numbers or digit strings — pick one
             in seed and stick to it (prefer numbers in JSON).
             ref = "BSB:John:3:16" always.

         LOOKUP (needs more than blind rows scan):
           (get 'bible-verse uuid) alone is not enough for humans.
           Provide ONE of (make picks; prefer smallest):
             A. Crud/store helper + prim:
                (find-row 'bible-verse "ref" "BSB:John:3:16")
                (find-rows 'bible-verse "abbrev" "John"
                           ["chapter" 3])  ; chapter list
             B. Dedicated thin prims bible-get-ref / bible-chapter
                (worse — special-case kernel).
           Prefer A (general field find). GIN on fields already exists;
           add UNIQUE index for idempotent seed:
             CREATE UNIQUE INDEX data_rows_bible_ref_uidx
               ON data_rows ((fields->>'ref'))
               WHERE type_name = 'bible-verse';
           (migration 004 additive — only if make needs it).

         LISP API (libs/bible/lib.pp):
           (bible/ref book chapter verse) -> "BSB:John:3:16"
             book may be name or abbrev; normalize via bible-book catalog
           (bible/verse book chapter verse) -> text VStr | VNil
           (bible/verse-row book chapter verse) -> fields JSON VStr | VNil
           (bible/chapter book chapter) -> VList of verse fields/sexps
           (bible/books) -> list from bible-book rows ordered by ordinal
           (bible/search q) -> VList hits (v1: SQL ILIKE or limited scan;
             may be prim-backed; OK if slow/limited first cut)
           (bible/page) -> GET renders chapter; POST goto redirects
           (route "/bible" "bible/page")

         URL SURFACE (exact path /bible only — no path params in v1):
           GET /bible
           GET /bible?book=John&chapter=3
           GET /bible?book=John&chapter=3&verse=16
           GET /bible?ref=BSB:John:3:16
           GET /bible?q=love                    (optional search)
           POST /bible  book= & chapter= & verse=
             -> redirect to GET with query (PRG)
           Do NOT require colon-ids in path segments.
           ref= query is the shareable explicit form (URL-ok in query).

         ABBREV TABLE (seed must use these tokens in ref):
           Gen Exod Lev Num Deut Josh Judg Ruth 1Sam 2Sam 1Kgs 2Kgs
           1Chr 2Chr Ezra Neh Esth Job Ps Prov Eccl Song Isa Jer Lam
           Ezek Dan Hos Joel Amos Obad Jonah Mic Nah Hab Zeph Hag
           Zech Mal
           Matt Mark Luke John Acts Rom 1Cor 2Cor Gal Eph Phil Col
           1Thess 2Thess 1Tim 2Tim Titus Phlm Heb Jas 1Pet 2Pet
           1John 2John 3John Jude Rev
           (If vendor data uses different codes, map in seed script;
            document mapping next to script. Display names full English.)

         BULK SEED SCRIPT (required — not HTTP recipe):
           Path: scripts/seed_bible_bsb.py  (or .ksh driver + py)
           Inputs:
             - BSB source file(s) (JSON/CSV/USFM — vendor format;
               script owns parsing; do not commit illegal redistrib if
               license forbids; prefer redistributable BSB dump + NOTICE)
             - PG connect: same as dev (PP_DB_* / /tmp:5433)
           Behavior:
             1. Ensure type_defs exist OR document that load-library bible
                must run first (deftype). Prefer: script can INSERT type_defs
                rows for bible-book/bible-verse if missing (author system).
             2. Upsert ~66 bible-book rows (stable id or fields.abbrev unique).
             3. Upsert all bible-verse rows:
                  fields.ref unique; ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE text
                  (requires unique index on ref — migration or special
                   conflict target).
             4. Idempotent: second run = no duplicate verses.
             5. Print counts: books N, verses M.
           NOT in recipe_bible HTTP handler (too slow / wrong layer).

         INSTALL STORY:
           A. scripts/seed_bible_bsb.py     # once per DB
           B. (load-library "bible")        # or POST /api/recipe/bible
           C. (bible/verse "John" 3 16)
           D. open /bible?book=John&chapter=3

         HOME UI (optional make):
           Card "Install bible library" -> POST /api/recipe/bible
           Note on card: "Run seed script for BSB text if empty".

         RENDER:
           v1: simple HTML chapter list + optional verse highlight is OK.
           Optional: ?view=sexp returns chapter as Lisp list via bare value.
           Attribution footer: Berean Standard Bible / license line.

         PERSONAL LAYER: notes/bookmarks are NOT bible/dabar — a
           separate module owns them. Canon text stays shared.

         MAKE CHECKLIST:
           1. Abbrev map + seed script I/O against sample or full BSB
           2. migration 004 unique index on verse ref (if upsert needs it)
           3. find-row / find-rows (or equivalent) for ref + chapter
           4. libs/bible/lib.pp API + route /bible
           5. recipe_bible + optional home card
           6. Smokes in verify stanza above

         OUT OF SCOPE V1:
           - multi-translation UI
           - path params /bible/John/3/16 (map later from same ref)
           - full-text search engine
           - audio / cross-refs apparatus
           - pure-sexp file corpus as runtime source of truth

         DEFERRED (packages general): library_files table, git export,
         multi-file manifests, load from HTTP.

         Kernel prims (OCaml) stay under (kernel) browser package.
         Majority of app code remains Lisp in .pp files.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection browser-repl
   (doc "Evaluate Lisp from the browser against the live session env")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "POST /api/eval (+ 20 22) => 42; define + call persists"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         - GET /repl — input + Eval button
         - POST /api/eval {expr} -> {ok,value|error}
         - Same Session.eval_one path as SSH REPL
         - Not a second interpreter
